---
title: "Clear High-Interest Debt Before You Save for a Down Payment"
description: "Saving hard for a down payment while carrying high-interest debt can actually shrink what you can buy. Here's why, and how to restructure first."
publishDate: 2026-11-18
category: first-time-buyers
readTime: 5
---

Many first-time buyers I meet are doing everything they've been told: cutting back, saving every month, watching the down payment fund grow. But they're also carrying a car loan, a couple of credit cards and a line of credit. In many cases, those debts are holding back their approval far more than their savings are helping it.

## How Lenders See Your Debts

When you apply for a mortgage, lenders calculate your debt service ratios, which compare your monthly obligations to your income. Your housing costs *plus* your other monthly debt payments have to fit under a limit, commonly around 44% of your gross income for the total debt ratio. Every dollar going to a car loan or credit card minimum is a dollar that can't go toward a mortgage payment.

**Illustration:** say you have $38,000 of consumer debt costing about $1,000 a month in payments. At a qualifying rate of 6.29% over 25 years, that $1,000 a month could support roughly **$150,000 of mortgage**. Clearing or restructuring that debt can increase what you qualify for by about that much. (Illustrative numbers only; your result depends on your income and other factors.)

## Why Saving Alone Can Be the Slower Road

* Your savings may earn a few percent while your cards charge 20% or more.
* A bigger down payment doesn't fix debt ratios that are too high.
* High card balances can also lower your credit score, which affects your rate.

## A Better Order of Operations

1. **List every debt** with its balance, rate and monthly payment.
2. **Target the debts with the biggest payments relative to their balance**, since those hurt your ratios most, along with the highest rates.
3. **Consider restructuring**, for example a lower-rate consolidation loan with a smaller monthly payment.
4. **Keep saving in the background,** ideally in an [FHSA](fhsa-rrsp-tfsa-down-payment-order.html) so you get the tax deduction.
5. **Get pre-approved** once your ratios are in good shape, so you know your real budget.

## A Word on Using Savings to Pay Off Debt

Sometimes the smartest move is to use part of your savings to clear a card or loan right away. Other times it's better to keep the cash. That depends on how close you are to buying, how much down payment you need and what the debts cost. It's worth running the numbers before you decide.

